WINSNAP(1)                General Commands Manual               WINSNAP(1)

NAME
     winsnap — nine-position window snapping for GNOME Shell

SYNOPSIS
     make install
     make enable
     make live

DESCRIPTION
     winsnap tiles the focused window into one of nine rectangles on its
     monitor's work area: vertical halves (left, right), horizontal
     halves (up, down), four quarters, and the full work area
     (maximize), plus minimize.  Pressing a position key a second time
     restores the window's previous geometry; pressing maximize twice
     restores as well.

     Fullscreen windows are left alone.  Dialogs and other non-normal
     windows are left alone.

     If a shortcut collides with a built-in GNOME shortcut (the stock
     Super+arrow tiling, maximize, minimize), the built-in one is
     disabled while winsnap is enabled and restored afterwards.  No
     other part of the GNOME configuration is ever modified.

KEYBINDINGS
     Defaults follow the well-known Super+arrow layout; halves and
     quarters follow the numpad compass.  All eleven are configurable
     in the settings dialog (Extensions → winsnap) or with gsettings(1):

           <Super>Left              left half
           <Super>Right             right half
           <Super>KP_8              top half
           <Super>KP_2              bottom half
           <Super>KP_7              top-left quarter
           <Super>KP_9              top-right quarter
           <Super>KP_1              bottom-left quarter
           <Super>KP_3              bottom-right quarter
           <Super>Up                maximize
           <Super>Down              restore (unmaximize, float centered)
           <Super>h                 minimize

     Laptops without a numpad may prefer, say,
     <Super><Ctrl>Left/Up/Right/Down for the quarters.  The schema
     lives with the extension, so gsettings needs --schemadir:

           gsettings --schemadir \
               ~/.local/share/gnome-shell/extensions/winsnap@apiratchai/schemas \
               set org.gnome.shell.extensions.winsnap \
               snap-top-left '<Super><Ctrl>Left'

     Set a key to the empty array to disable that shortcut.

     Each action accepts several accelerators.  The settings dialog
     adds to the list; to remove individual ones or set several at
     once, use gsettings:

           gsettings --schemadir \
               ~/.local/share/gnome-shell/extensions/winsnap@apiratchai/schemas \
               set org.gnome.shell.extensions.winsnap snap-down \
               "['<Super>Down', '<Super>2']"

     A rebinding that collides with a built-in shortcut (for example
     <Super>2, the app switcher) disables that built-in for as long as
     winsnap is enabled.

D-BUS
     The extension exports the interface org.gnome.Shell.Extensions.Winsnap
     on the session bus at /org/gnome/shell/extensions/winsnap (under the
     shell's own bus name), so windows can be tiled from scripts:

           SnapWindow(title, position)        -> found
           MoveWindow(title, x, y, w, h)      -> found
           GetWindowRect(title)               -> "x y w h" | gone
           GetWindowState(title)              -> normal|minimized|maximized|fullscreen|gone
           GetMonitorWorkArea(title)          -> "x y w h" | gone
           SetFullscreen(title, full)         -> found
           GetMonitors()                      -> count

     Position is one of left, right, up, down, top_left, top_right,
     bottom_left, bottom_right, maximize, minimize.  tests/test.sh
     drives the same interface.

FILES
     ~/.local/share/gnome-shell/extensions/winsnap@apiratchai/

TESTING
     make unit   — geometry checks, no shell needed
     make live   — live tests against the running shell (spawns ptyxis
                   and zenity windows; the extension must be enabled)

LIMITATIONS
     The shell scans for extensions only at session start; a new
     install needs a log out and back in.

     tiling-assistant@leleat-on-github and other tiling extensions
     grab the same keys; disable them (gnome-extensions disable
     tiling-assistant@leleat-on-github) or rebind winsnap.

     GNOME 50 has no programmatic way to simulate key presses from
     outside the shell, so the live suite verifies the snap geometry
     and state transitions, not the key grab itself.

BUGS
     Report to the author.  Windows tiled by drag-and-drop keep a tile
     constraint; snapping them away with winsnap works, but GNOME may
     re-assert the constraint on later resizes.  This is a mutter
     behaviour we do not fight.

